Decide whether these triangles are similar. Find the unmarked angle in each triangle and compare corresponding sides.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(2.4,0) node[midway,below] {$12\text{ cm}$}--(2.4,1) node[midway,right] {$5\text{ cm}$}--cycle;
\path (1.2,0.5) node[above left] {$13\text{ cm}$};
\draw (2.2,0)--(2.2,0.2)--(2.4,0.2);
\path (0.65,0.13) node {$23^{\circ}$};
\draw (4,0)--(7.6,0) node[midway,below] {$18\text{ cm}$}--(7.6,1.5) node[midway,right] {$7.5\text{ cm}$}--cycle;
\path (5.8,0.75) node[above left] {$19.5\text{ cm}$};
\draw (7.4,0)--(7.4,0.2)--(7.6,0.2);
\path (4.7,0.13) node {$23^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The marked acute angles are rounded to the nearest degree.

Are these rectangles similar? Compare corresponding sides and explain why equal angles alone do not settle the question.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (2.4,1.2);
\path (1.2,0) node[below] {$8\text{ cm}$} (0,0.6) node[left] {$4\text{ cm}$};
\draw (4,0) rectangle (7.6,2.4);
\path (5.8,0) node[below] {$12\text{ cm}$} (7.6,1.2) node[right] {$8\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The corresponding angles of these pentagons are equal. Decide whether the pentagons are similar by checking their corresponding sides.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,1.2)--(0,0) node[midway,left] {$4$}--(1.8,0) node[midway,below] {$6$}--(1.8,1.2) node[midway,right] {$4$}--(0.9,2.4) node[midway,above right] {$5$}--cycle;
\path (0.45,1.8) node[above left] {$5$};
\draw (4,1.8)--(4,0) node[midway,left] {$6$}--(7.6,0) node[midway,below] {$12$}--(7.6,1.8) node[midway,right] {$6$}--(5.8,4.2) node[midway,above right] {$10$}--cycle;
\path (4.9,3) node[above left] {$10$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Rectangles $A$ and $B$ are drawn on a grid of unit squares. Is $B$ similar to $A$? Explain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[step=4mm,color={rgb,255:red,204;green,204;blue,204},thin] (0,0) grid (10,10);
\draw[thick] (1,1) rectangle (5,3);
\draw[thick] (6,1) rectangle (10,9);
\path (3,2) node {$A$} (8,5) node {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Triangles $X$ and $Y$ are drawn on a grid of unit squares. A student says $Y$ is an enlargement of $X$. Is the student correct? Explain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=5mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[step=5mm,color={rgb,255:red,204;green,204;blue,204},thin] (0,0) grid (11,4);
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(4,0)--(2,3)--cycle;
\draw[thick] (5,0)--(11,0)--(8,3)--cycle;
\path (2,1) node {$X$} (8,1) node {$Y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
